---
/**
 * The site's homepage.
 *
 * This is a "custom page" in Starlight's sense: a file under `src/pages/` rendered with the
 * `<StarlightPage>` component, rather than a page in the `docs` content collection. Astro's
 * file-based routing gives files in `src/pages/` priority over Starlight's own collection-driven
 * routes, so this file owns "/" outright — `scripts/sync-docs.mjs` writes its synced copy of the
 * SDK's README to `readme.md`, not `index.md`, specifically so the two never contend for the same
 * route. See that script's comment on `TOP_LEVEL_FILE_ROUTES` for the full reasoning.
 *
 * Content below is hand-written for this site; it is not sourced from `docs/` and is not touched by
 * the sync script.
 */
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import { CardGrid, LinkCard, Aside, Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';

const provenanceExample = `import { useCPU } from 'pixelkit';

const { cpuLoadPercent, source } = useCPU();

// source: 'hardware' | 'derived' | 'unavailable' — never 'simulated'.
// cpuLoadPercent is null when cpufreq sysfs can't be read, not a guess.
return <Text>{cpuLoadPercent ?? '—'}%</Text>;`;

const installExample = `npx expo install pixelkit @pixelkit/native

# optional, separate: on-device Gemini Nano (+19 ML Kit artifacts)
npx expo install @pixelkit/mlkit`;

const importExample = `import { useCPU } from 'pixelkit';
import { useGeminiNano } from 'pixelkit/mlkit';`;
---

<StarlightPage
	frontmatter={{
		title: 'PixelKit',
		description:
			'Typed React hooks for real Google Pixel hardware and on-device AI, each one telling you whether its value is real.',
		template: 'splash',
		hero: {
			title: 'Real hardware. No pretending.',
			tagline:
				'32 typed React hooks for the Google Pixel: per-core CPU frequencies, battery temperature, camera, radios, biometrics, the hardware keystore, and Gemini Nano on-device.',
			actions: [
				{ text: 'Get started', link: '/getting-started/quickstart/', variant: 'primary', icon: 'right-arrow' },
				{ text: 'API reference', link: '/api/hardware-api/', variant: 'secondary' },
			],
		},
	}}
>
	<div class="sl-markdown-content">
		<h2>Every value says where it came from</h2>
		<p>
			Every hook returns a <code>source</code> field typed as
			<code>'hardware' | 'derived' | 'unavailable'</code>. There is no <code>'simulated'</code> member —
			the type makes a fabricated reading unrepresentable. When a value can't be read, it is
			<code>null</code>, it renders as an em dash, and <code>source</code> says
			<code>'unavailable'</code>, so the caller can tell the difference and the UI can refuse
			rather than show a plausible-looking number that isn't real.
		</p>

		<Code code={provenanceExample} lang="tsx" title="any component" />

		<h2>Install</h2>
		<p>
			The core SDK and the native telemetry module install together. On-device inference is a
			separate, opt-in package, because it adds 19 ML Kit artifacts to the built APK.
		</p>

		<Code code={installExample} lang="sh" />

		<p>Two entry points, both typed:</p>

		<Code code={importExample} lang="tsx" />

		<Aside type="caution" title="Before you install">
			<p>
				PixelKit is Android only — there is no iOS build. It also cannot run inside Expo Go:
				reading a thermal sensor or a fuel-gauge register takes native code compiled into the app,
				so every project needs a development build (<code>npx expo run:android</code> or an EAS dev
				client).
			</p>
		</Aside>

		<h2>Where to go</h2>

		<CardGrid>
			<LinkCard
				title="Quickstart"
				description="Install the SDK, run a development build, read your first hook."
				href="/getting-started/quickstart/"
			/>
			<LinkCard
				title="API reference"
				description="All 32 hooks: arguments, return fields, units, and failure modes."
				href="/api/hardware-api/"
			/>
			<LinkCard
				title="Guides"
				description="On-device Gemini Nano, function calling over hardware, voice, troubleshooting."
				href="/guides/"
			/>
			<LinkCard
				title="For coding agents"
				description="The rules an agent follows in a PixelKit project, and the hook table it needs."
				href="/for-coding-agents/ai-primer/"
			/>
		</CardGrid>
	</div>
</StarlightPage>
